1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,010
So far, you've written who lives in your universe,

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:06,616
and the game has decided what every system looks like.

3
00:00:07,516 --> 00:00:09,703
Today you take some of that back.

4
00:00:11,503 --> 00:00:15,324
You'll change how the whole galaxy is made, with four numbers.

5
00:00:16,224 --> 00:00:19,519
You'll give two stretches of it a name and a sky.

6
00:00:20,419 --> 00:00:22,999
And you'll put three more places on the map by hand.

7
00:00:24,199 --> 00:00:28,314
First, how the game fills a system you never wrote.

8
00:00:29,214 --> 00:00:30,875
It rolls once for each one,

9
00:00:31,325 --> 00:00:32,935
and the roll comes from the seed,

10
00:00:33,385 --> 00:00:35,951
so it's the same every time for that system.

11
00:00:37,751 --> 00:00:39,950
About one in seven is a station.

12
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,117
About one in seven is full of enemies.

13
00:00:45,017 --> 00:00:48,004
Some are nebula, a few are anomalies,

14
00:00:48,454 --> 00:00:50,189
and half of them are empty.

15
00:00:51,989 --> 00:00:54,934
And on top of that, a system might have loot,

16
00:00:55,384 --> 00:00:58,094
or a dead ship to scan, or a minefield.

17
00:00:58,994 --> 00:01:02,097
Every one of those chances is a number you can change.

18
00:01:03,297 --> 00:01:05,506
Those numbers are called dials,

19
00:01:05,956 --> 00:01:09,150
and they go in the title record, at the top of the file.

20
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:13,704
My universe is a frontier that was abandoned.

21
00:01:14,604 --> 00:01:18,359
So I want few ports, a lot of cloud,

22
00:01:18,809 --> 00:01:20,232
and a lot of dead ships,

23
00:01:20,682 --> 00:01:22,258
and that's four lines.

24
00:01:24,058 --> 00:01:28,963
And here's what they did, counted over the nearest hundred and sixty-nine systems.

25
00:01:29,863 --> 00:01:32,810
Stations went from twenty-five to fifteen,

26
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:35,828
and nebulas from twenty-nine to forty.

27
00:01:37,628 --> 00:01:40,091
One warning, and it's the big one in this lecture.

28
00:01:40,991 --> 00:01:42,402
Write the percent sign.

29
00:01:43,302 --> 00:01:46,494
Leave it off, and twelve means twelve hundred percent,

30
00:01:46,944 --> 00:01:48,602
and lint won't say a word.

31
00:01:49,802 --> 00:01:52,464
A dial in the title record is the same everywhere.

32
00:01:53,364 --> 00:01:56,202
A region is a patch of the map where it's different.

33
00:01:58,002 --> 00:02:01,050
It has a center, which is a system, and a radius.

34
00:02:01,950 --> 00:02:04,815
A region is a square, so a radius of two

35
00:02:05,265 --> 00:02:07,303
is five systems on a side.

36
00:02:09,103 --> 00:02:12,193
It has a sky, from a list of seven on the page.

37
00:02:13,093 --> 00:02:15,637
And it can have any of the dials.

38
00:02:16,537 --> 00:02:20,567
Here I've said, no enemy systems at all, and more stations.

39
00:02:21,467 --> 00:02:25,237
So this is safe, farmed space around the home port.

40
00:02:26,437 --> 00:02:28,475
And here's the opposite: the Breakers.

41
00:02:29,375 --> 00:02:31,502
There's a red sky, different music,

42
00:02:31,952 --> 00:02:35,401
enemies in most systems, no ports, and mines.

43
00:02:37,201 --> 00:02:38,743
I drew it on paper first,

44
00:02:39,193 --> 00:02:41,256
and I'd ask you to do the same.

45
00:02:42,156 --> 00:02:45,761
Mark each side's home, and draw each region as a square.

46
00:02:47,561 --> 00:02:48,995
It shows you two things.

47
00:02:49,895 --> 00:02:52,264
One is whether two regions overlap.

48
00:02:52,714 --> 00:02:56,787
If they do, a system belongs to the one written first.

49
00:02:57,687 --> 00:03:01,721
The other is whether a side's home is inside the region you meant for it.

50
00:03:02,621 --> 00:03:05,381
Nothing moves a home into a region for you.

51
00:03:05,831 --> 00:03:08,347
You keep those two lines in step yourself.

52
00:03:09,547 --> 00:03:10,814
Now for the landmarks.

53
00:03:11,714 --> 00:03:13,308
You wrote one in Lecture 2,

54
00:03:13,758 --> 00:03:17,192
and here are three more, each for a different reason.

55
00:03:18,992 --> 00:03:21,525
The first is a station at the miners' home.

56
00:03:22,425 --> 00:03:24,750
Last time, when the crew arrived there,

57
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,402
the card just said uncharted, and two numbers.

58
00:03:29,302 --> 00:03:31,388
A side's home doesn't name itself,

59
00:03:31,838 --> 00:03:33,355
but a landmark does.

60
00:03:35,155 --> 00:03:38,151
So now the card will read Assay Office,

61
00:03:38,601 --> 00:03:41,870
and the place goes on the crew's list of charted locations.

62
00:03:42,770 --> 00:03:45,122
And because of this line, Side,

63
00:03:45,572 --> 00:03:48,693
the station belongs to the miners and offers their work.

64
00:03:49,893 --> 00:03:52,008
The second is a dead ship.

65
00:03:52,908 --> 00:03:56,646
Prefab, derelict, makes it a wreck for Science to scan.

66
00:03:57,546 --> 00:04:00,568
The game scatters wrecks of its own, but they have no names.

67
00:04:01,468 --> 00:04:04,379
This one has a name, a line on the card,

68
00:04:04,829 --> 00:04:07,712
and a nebula around it, whatever the game rolled there.

69
00:04:09,512 --> 00:04:11,303
And the third is a fight.

70
00:04:12,203 --> 00:04:14,168
Guards puts a fleet on the landmark,

71
00:04:14,618 --> 00:04:16,635
the first time the crew arrives.

72
00:04:18,435 --> 00:04:19,846
One thing to know about them.

73
00:04:20,746 --> 00:04:22,376
The guards belong to nobody.

74
00:04:22,826 --> 00:04:25,433
They're raiders, even in Gleaner country,

75
00:04:25,883 --> 00:04:29,020
so a ceasefire with the Gleaners won't call them off.

76
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:32,812
The crew still needs a reason to go,

77
00:04:33,262 --> 00:04:36,560
so I add two leads, the way we did in Lecture 3.

78
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:39,370
The Assay Office doesn't need one,

79
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:41,937
because an old lead already goes there.

80
00:04:43,737 --> 00:04:46,103
The third colony gets two paragraphs,

81
00:04:46,553 --> 00:04:48,096
with an empty line between them.

82
00:04:48,996 --> 00:04:51,190
The Quest Log shows a lead as a page,

83
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:54,480
so the first paragraph is the order,

84
00:04:54,930 --> 00:04:57,065
and the second is why anyone would go.

85
00:04:58,865 --> 00:05:00,867
A landmark's line is different.

86
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:03,498
The card shows the first line,

87
00:05:03,948 --> 00:05:06,393
and cuts it after sixty characters,

88
00:05:06,843 --> 00:05:10,180
so no landmark line here is longer than that.

89
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:14,144
I save, and lint says clean.

90
00:05:15,044 --> 00:05:18,011
And again, clean isn't the whole check today.

91
00:05:18,911 --> 00:05:21,768
Lint doesn't know a sky's name from a typing mistake,

92
00:05:22,218 --> 00:05:24,352
and it doesn't count your percent signs.

93
00:05:25,252 --> 00:05:27,379
So read down the list on the page.

94
00:05:28,579 --> 00:05:30,871
Four leads in the Quest Log now.

95
00:05:31,771 --> 00:05:33,343
First, the third colony.

96
00:05:33,793 --> 00:05:36,478
There's her name on the card, and my line.

97
00:05:38,278 --> 00:05:39,348
Then the miners' home,

98
00:05:39,798 --> 00:05:41,751
and this time it has a name.

99
00:05:42,651 --> 00:05:46,461
On Comms, the Assay Office offers the miners' escort.

100
00:05:48,261 --> 00:05:50,187
Now the one the Gleaners won't talk about.

101
00:05:51,087 --> 00:05:53,603
This is the Breakers, and somebody's waiting.

102
00:05:55,403 --> 00:05:57,532
And last, the Gleaners' own home.

103
00:05:58,432 --> 00:05:59,755
It has no landmark,

104
00:06:00,205 --> 00:06:02,809
so the card falls back to the region's name.

105
00:06:04,609 --> 00:06:07,491
Here's Charted Locations, with four places in it.

106
00:06:08,391 --> 00:06:11,051
Those are my landmarks, and only my landmarks.

107
00:06:11,951 --> 00:06:13,445
A region doesn't get charted,

108
00:06:13,895 --> 00:06:16,400
and neither does a home with no landmark in it.

109
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:18,603
Now it's your turn.

110
00:06:19,503 --> 00:06:22,414
Say what your galaxy is like in one sentence,

111
00:06:22,864 --> 00:06:25,429
and set no more than four dials to match.

112
00:06:26,329 --> 00:06:27,970
Draw the grid on paper.

113
00:06:28,870 --> 00:06:30,985
Give every side's home a landmark,

114
00:06:31,435 --> 00:06:35,532
and write one place that's only a story, and one that's a fight.

115
00:06:37,332 --> 00:06:41,490
Next time, the work a station hands out gets a beginning and an end.
